Evidence 2 (Mr. Knight's Body) states that "Death would have been instant" from a stab wound to the throat. However, Security Camera Footage (Evidence 15) shows the victim being attacked in his holding cell. Meanwhile, Testimony 3 (Sahwit) claims: "Then I saw him lying there... A man... not moving... dead!" suggesting the body was discovered lying still in a workshop. If death were truly instantaneous (Evidence 2), the victim could not have been attacked, moved, and then found dead later as described by Sahwit unless the timelines align perfectly, which the evidence (including autopsy and time of death) makes questionable.

Most notably, if Sahwit claims to have seen the dead body in the workshop (Testimony 3), but Security Camera Footage (Evidence 15) shows him being attacked in his cell and not in the workshop, this creates a contradiction between where, when, and how the body was discovered or where the murder actually took place.

